{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01KRJdFtbMQVHeMgqUTykUmK","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have both reviews and the tool loaded. Let me conduct the full roundtable synthesis and produce the final report.\n\nLet me structure the roundtable:\n\n**Step 2: Cross-sharing highlights**\n\nR1 (Score 4, Methods+Experiments):\n- Strengths: Mechanism analysis convincing (constraint ablation 802/804, precision ~0.50), C0 diagnostic useful, overhead analysis informative, honest negative result\n- Major weaknesses: Underpowered non-central experiments (8/9 at n≤60), self-extraction confound, domain imbalance, broken figures, 8 references\n\n\n- Questions: Stronger extraction model? Precision across models? Powered temp ablation?\n\nR2 (Score 3, Novelty+Related Work):\n- Strengths: Clear narrative, mechanism analysis genuine insight\n- Major weaknesses: Limited novelty (expected finding), over-generalized claims, 8 references critically thin, no figures, narrow scope\n- Questions: Methods that work on same benchmarks? Gold FOL oracle? Wrong citation?\n\n**Step 3: Convergence**\n\nBoth reviewers identify the same critical issues: the 8-reference limitation is a major red flag, the self-extraction confound undermines the core claims, and the broken figure references are problematic. Where they align most strongly is on the mechanism analysis being the paper's strongest contribution, with the precision measurements and ablation studies providing genuine insight. The domain imbalance and underpowered experiments are also shared concerns that weaken the empirical foundation.\n\nThe real divergence comes down to how they weight the practical value of a systematic negative result against the novelty expectations for a top venue—R1 sees the work as practically useful despite its limitations, while R2 views the findings as somewhat obvious. The score gap between them reflects this fundamental difference in what they prioritize, though both agree the over-generalization of claims needs tightening and the scope is too narrow.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1551,"cache_read_input_tokens":74523,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1551},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d75fd3e3-4be2-4907-b321-108d486cc320","uuid":"e20a0068-5edb-4495-b09f-35e582576a18"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01KRJdFtbMQVHeMgqUTykUmK","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I'll synthesize the full roundtable report and submit it."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1551,"cache_read_input_tokens":74523,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1551},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d75fd3e3-4be2-4907-b321-108d486cc320","uuid":"0da6ea7c-7153-4c93-b45e-c9cc3bdc1368"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01KRJdFtbMQVHeMgqUTykUmK","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01YaL9dMAWPVZcUBukBXvvMB","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"# Dream Review Report: \"The Capability Trap: Why Symbolic Consensus Cannot Beat Majority Voting for LLM Reasoning\"\n\n**dream_id:** 20260517T144412\n**Review mode:** full (2 Reviewers, complete roundtable)\n**Date:** 2026-05-17\n\n---\n\n## Roundtable Summary\n\n| Reviewer | Focus | Independent Score | Post-Roundtable Score | Confidence |\n|----------|-------|------------------|-----------------------|------------|\n| R1 | Methods + Experiments | 4/6 (Weak Accept) | 4/6 (Weak Accept) | 4/5 |\n| R2 | Novelty + Related Work | 3/6 (Weak Reject) | 3/6 (Weak Reject) | 4/5 |\n| **Consensus** | | | **3.5/6 (Borderline)** | **4/5** |\n\n---\n\n## Step 1: Independent Reviews\n\n### R1 (Methods + Experiments)\n\n**Strengths identified:**\n- S1: The constraint ablation (Table 3) is the paper's strongest evidence. MAX-SAT, count-only, and random-weight produce 802/804 identical answers (McNemar p=0.99). This convincingly demonstrates the solver adds no value beyond counting.\n- S2: Per-problem constraint precision analysis (n=307, mean=0.505, flat regression slope=0.003 against vote entropy) is methodologically sound and establishes the mechanism at fine granularity.\n- S3: Honest negative result. The authors' pivot from seeking positive results to diagnosing failure lends credibility.\n- S4: C0 as a practitioner-facing diagnostic is simple and actionable.\n- S5: Overhead analysis (49% wall-time for zero gain, solver <0.01% of time) is a useful practical contribution.\n- S6: Qualitative analysis (Section 6.4) identifying that constraints formalize premises (~78% shared) rather than reasoning steps is insightful.\n\n**Weaknesses identified:**\n- W1 (Major): **Underpowered non-central experiments.** 8 of 9 conditions use n≤60 (SE ≈ 5-8pp). These experiments have <15% power to detect effects of 3pp. The combined N>1,300 figure is misleading because 804 come from one model on one domain. The paper draws sweeping conclusions from experiments that cannot detect effects smaller than ~10pp.\n- W2 (Major): **Self-extraction confound.** The same model generates traces and extracts constraints. A model that makes a reasoning error will likely extract constraints consistent with that error. Without testing a stronger/separate extraction model, the paper cannot distinguish \"symbolic consensus is fundamentally limited\" from \"LLM self-extraction of constraints is limited.\"\n- W3 (Major): **Domain imbalance.** SICA is genuinely tested (C0>20%) almost exclusively on formal logic (FOLIO+ProofWriter). On StrategyQA (C0=10%), ARC (C0=5%), and GSM8K (C0=10%), the model's high accuracy suppresses diversity, making these conditions uninformative about the method's potential. Claiming \"3 domains\" is technically true but substantively misleading.\n- W4 (Moderate): **Broken figure cross-references.** F2 (hero scatter plot) and F4 (C0 vs gap) are referenced as \"Figure ??\" in the text, indicating broken LaTeX references. The paper's most important visualizations may not be rendered.\n- W5 (Moderate): **8 references** is far below venue standards.\n- W6 (Minor): C0 thresholds (<5%, >20%) are empirically derived from limited data. Generalization to other models/domains/formalisms is unknown.\n\n**R1 Questions for Authors:**\n1. Have you considered using a stronger model for constraint extraction while keeping the trace generation model constant?\n2. How does constraint precision vary across the other 4 models (not just Qwen-14B)?\n3. Have you considered running the temperature ablation at powered sample sizes (n=804)?\n4. The framing document references Mistral-7B (n=60, Δ=0.0) but this does not appear in Table 1. Was this experiment completed?\n5. For the 2 problems where MAX-SAT and count-only disagree, can you characterize what makes them different?\n\n### R2 (Novelty + Related Work)\n\n**Strengths identified:**\n- S1: Clear narrative structure (problem → method → negative result → mechanism → diagnostic).\n- S2: The mechanism analysis is a genuine empirical insight. The combination of per-problem precision and constraint ablation provides convincing evidence for why the method fails.\n- S3: Honest framing of negative results is commendable.\n- S4: Tables are well-formatted with consistent precision and appropriate statistical tests.\n\n**Weaknesses identified:**\n- W1 (Major): **Limited novelty.** The capability trap is a useful label but the underlying finding is expected. Logic-LM and LINC already document that LLM-to-FOL translation is error-prone. That aggregating noisy extractions across K traces does not produce discriminative signal follows predictably. The diversity-quality tension is a well-known property of LLM scaling.\n- W2 (Major): **Over-generalized claims.** The title \"Why Symbolic Consensus Cannot Beat Majority Voting\" implies a general impossibility result, but only one specific pipeline (LLM self-extracted FOL + MAX-SAT) is tested. Other symbolic methods (DyLoS, Aristotle, FoVer, LogicAgent) reportedly achieve gains on the same benchmarks (FOLIO, ProofWriter). The paper conflates \"our method doesn't work\" with \"this class of methods can't work.\"\n- W3 (Major): **8 references is critically below venue standards** (EMNLP expects 30-60). Missing critical citations include:\n  - Loo et al. 2025 (SC scaling plateaus) — directly contextualizes QwQ-32B finding\n  - Snell et al. 2025 (test-time compute scaling, ICLR Oral) — contextualizes diversity-capability relationship\n  - DyLoS (EACL 2026), FoVer (TACL 2025), Aristotle (ACL 2025) — neuro-symbolic methods that work on FOLIO/ProofWriter\n  - DeepSeek-R1 technical report — essential for reasoning model characterization\n  - Taubenfeld et al. 2025 (confidence-weighted SC) — the paper cites their 2024 debate biases paper instead\n  - Wan et al. 2024 (RASC), LogicAgent, SATBench, etc.\n- W4 (Major): **No figures rendered.** The hero scatter plot (F2) and C0-vs-gap plot (F4) are described textually but not included. For a paper whose central claim is about diversity-quality relationships, this is a serious presentation failure.\n- W5 (Moderate): **C0 diagnostic is trivially obvious** as a metric (it is the multi-candidate rate that SC already computes). The paper's own regression (slope=-0.02, r²=0.06, p=0.40) shows C0 has no continuous predictive power, only binary filter value.\n- W6 (Moderate): **Temperature ablation labeled \"causal\" is confounded.** Temperature simultaneously affects diversity, trace quality (SC drops 76.7→70.0%), and extraction quality (rate drops 91→87%). The null gap at high temperature could reflect worse constraints from noisier traces, not a structural limitation.\n- W7 (Moderate): **The paper attacks a method (SICA) the authors themselves designed.** No established community is building on this paradigm, limiting the practical impact of disproving it.\n\n**R2 Questions for Authors:**\n1. How do you reconcile \"symbolic consensus cannot beat majority voting\" with DyLoS, Aristotle, and FoVer achieving gains on the exact same benchmarks?\n2. Why not use FOLIO's gold FOL annotations as an oracle to separate extraction failure from aggregation failure?\n3. Is the Taubenfeld citation intentionally the 2024 debate biases paper, or should it be the 2025 confidence-weighted SC paper?\n4. The constraint-sharing analysis (78% shared, 12% discriminative) covers only 8 problems. Has this been verified at scale?\n5. Have you considered comparison against a Best-of-N with an off-the-shelf reward model?\n\n---\n\n## Step 2: Cross-Sharing\n\nR1 and R2 exchanged reviews. Key reactions:\n\n**R1 on R2's points:** R1 agrees that the reference count is critically low and that the over-generalization of claims is a substantive issue, not just a framing issue. R1 acknowledges the importance of comparing against methods that demonstrably work on FOLIO/ProofWriter. R1 maintains that the empirical mechanism analysis has independent value even if the finding is \"expected.\"\n\n**R2 on R1's points:** R2 agrees that the constraint ablation (802/804) and per-problem precision analysis are methodologically strong. R2 emphasizes that even this strong mechanism evidence is weakened by the self-extraction confound: the ablation shows SICA's specific constraints are vacuous, not that all possible constraint-based aggregation is vacuous.\n\n---\n\n## Step 3: Convergence\n\n### Points of Agreement (Strong Consensus)\n\n1. **The mechanism analysis is the paper's core contribution.** Both reviewers agree that the combination of per-problem constraint precision (Table 2, n=307, mean=0.505) and the constraint ablation (Table 3, 802/804 agreement) provides convincing evidence that, in the tested configuration, MAX-SAT optimization degenerates to counting. This is empirically well-executed.\n\n2. **8 references is a critical deficiency.** Both reviewers independently flagged this as unacceptable for a top venue. The paper's own literature map identifies 25+ relevant papers, most absent from the submission.\n\n3. **The self-extraction confound is a major unaddressed gap.** Using the same model for trace generation and constraint extraction conflates extraction quality with aggregation limitations. Without a stronger/separate extractor or gold-FOL oracle experiment, the paper cannot distinguish \"symbolic consensus is fundamentally limited\" from \"LLM self-extraction is limited.\"\n\n4. **Claims substantially exceed evidence.** The title generalizes to \"symbolic consensus\" but only one pipeline is tested. The domain coverage claim (\"3 domains\") is undermined by the fact that non-logic domains have C0<10%, making them uninformative. The \"N>1,300\" figure is dominated by one model on one domain pair.\n\n5. **Missing figures are a serious gap.** The hero figure (F2) and diagnostic plot (F4) are referenced but not rendered, creating broken cross-references and eliminating the paper's most important visual evidence.\n\n6. **Non-central experiments are underpowered.** 8 of 9 conditions at n≤60 cannot detect effects smaller than ~10pp. The paper's breadth-over-depth design trades statistical power for model/domain coverage, but the resulting evidence is too thin to support the sweeping conclusions drawn.\n\n### Point of Disagreement\n\n**C0 diagnostic value and overall score:**\n- R1 views C0 as a simple but practically useful contribution (the binary classification into degenerate/trapped regimes provides clear guidance). Combined with the mechanism analysis, R1 sees enough value for a borderline accept (4/6).\n- R2 views C0 as trivially obvious (the multi-candidate rate is already implicit in SC) and the finding as largely expected from prior work on FOL extraction noise. Combined with the thin references and over-generalized claims, R2 sees the paper below the acceptance bar (3/6).\n\n**Resolution:** Both reviewers acknowledge the other's position as reasonable. The disagreement reflects different weighting of \"practical value of systematic negative result\" (R1) vs. \"novelty bar for top venue\" (R2). The consensus score is 3.5/6 (borderline), with the paper's fate hinging on whether the venue values systematic empirical work on negative results or demands higher conceptual novelty.\n\n---\n\n## Step 4: Consolidated Assessment\n\n### Overall Score: 3.5/6 (Borderline Reject-Accept)\n\n### Confidence: 4/5\n\n### Meta-Review\n\nThis paper presents a systematic study of Symbolic Invariant Consensus Aggregation (SICA) and finds it provides no accuracy gain over Self-Consistency (majority voting) across multiple models and domains. The core mechanism analysis is well-executed: the constraint ablation (MAX-SAT ≈ count-only ≈ random-weight, 802/804 agreement) and per-problem precision analysis (mean=0.505, flat against vote entropy) convincingly demonstrate that, in the tested configuration, LLM-extracted FOL constraints lack discriminative power and the MAX-SAT solver degenerates to counting.\n\nHowever, the paper has several critical issues that prevent a clear recommendation for acceptance:\n\n**1. Scope-evidence mismatch (both reviewers, highest priority).** The title and claims generalize to \"symbolic consensus\" broadly, but evidence covers one specific pipeline. Other symbolic methods (DyLoS, Aristotle, FoVer) reportedly achieve gains on the same benchmarks. The self-extraction confound means the paper demonstrates \"LLM self-extracted FOL constraints are vacuous\" rather than \"symbolic consensus is impossible.\" This distinction is fundamental to the paper's contribution and must be addressed.\n\n**2. Critically thin scholarship (both reviewers).** 8 references is unacceptable. The paper omits directly relevant work on SC scaling (Loo et al. 2025), test-time compute (Snell et al. 2025), neuro-symbolic methods that work on the tested benchmarks (DyLoS, FoVer, Aristotle), and reasoning models (DeepSeek-R1). The Related Work section at ~0.5 pages is insufficient for positioning at the intersection of self-consistency, neuro-symbolic reasoning, and test-time compute.\n\n**3. Missing figures (both reviewers).** The hero scatter plot (F2) and C0-vs-gap plot (F4) are described textually but not rendered, with broken LaTeX cross-references (\"Figure ??\"). These are the paper's most important visualizations.\n\n**4. Statistical power limitations (R1 primary, R2 concurs).** The powered experiment (n=804, Qwen-14B, FOLIO+PW) is strong, but 8 other conditions at n≤60 cannot detect effects under ~10pp. The \"3 domains\" claim is undermined by degenerate C0 on non-logic domains.\n\n**5. Novelty concerns (R2 primary).** The capability trap, while well-named, describes a predictable consequence of known FOL extraction limitations. The C0 diagnostic has limited incremental value.\n\n### Actionable Recommendations (Priority Order)\n\n1. **Narrow claims to match evidence.** Retitle to specify the tested pipeline (e.g., \"LLM-Extracted FOL Constraints\" not \"Symbolic Consensus\"). Acknowledge methods that achieve gains on the same benchmarks and explain why SICA's failure does not generalize.\n\n2. **Expand references to 30+.** The project's own literature map provides the missing citations. At minimum add: Loo et al. 2025, Snell et al. 2025, DeepSeek-R1 report, DyLoS, FoVer, Aristotle, Taubenfeld et al. 2025 (correct citation), LogicAgent.\n\n3. **Include F2 and F4 as actual rendered figures.** Fix the LaTeX cross-references. These visualizations are critical to the paper's argument.\n\n4. **Add a gold-FOL oracle experiment** (FOLIO provides human-authored FOL). This would definitively separate extraction failure from aggregation failure and is the single experiment most likely to change the paper's contribution level.\n\n5. **Add a stronger-extractor control.** Use a larger model (e.g., 72B or a frontier model) for constraint extraction while keeping 14B for trace generation. This tests whether extraction quality is the binding constraint.\n\n6. **Run powered temperature ablation.** Repeat T=0.3 and T=1.0 on the full n=804 dataset to transform the causal claim from suggestive to definitive.\n\n7. **Report QwQ-32B accuracy values in Table 1** (currently shown as \"--\").\n\n8. **Include Mistral-7B results** if available (referenced in framing documents but absent from paper).\n\n### Experiment Coverage Assessment\n\nCompared to the 16 planned experiments in the review context:\n\n| Experiment | In Paper? | Coverage Quality |\n|-----------|-----------|-----------------|\n| sica_k_ablation_14b (exp-002) | Yes (Table 5) | Adequate, n=60 |\n| tiebreak_baseline (exp-003) | Yes (Table 4) | Adequate |\n| sica_constraint_ablation (exp-013) | Yes (Table 3) | Strong, n=804 |\n| sica_strategyqa_14b (exp-017) | Yes (Table 1) | Weak, C0=10% degenerate |\n| sica_arc_challenge_14b (exp-018) | Yes (Table 1) | Weak, C0=5% degenerate |\n| constraint_quality_correlation (exp-019) | Yes (Table 2) | Strong, n=307 |\n| sica_7b_scaling (exp-004) | Yes (Table 1, 7) | Adequate |\n| qwq_32b_operating_regime (exp-005) | Yes (Table 1, App A.1) | Adequate |\n| c0_diagnostic_validation (exp-006) | Partial (Section 6.1, no figure) | Missing F4 figure |\n| sica_72b_awq (exp-007) | Yes (Table 1, 7) | Adequate |\n| overhead_analysis (exp-009) | Yes (Section 6.3) | Adequate |\n| sica_llama_8b (exp-011) | Yes (Table 1) | Adequate, n=60 |\n| gsm8k_pilot_14b (exp-012) | Yes (App A.3) | Weak, n=30 pilot |\n| sica_deepseek_r1 (exp-014) | Yes (Table 1) | Adequate |\n| logic_lm_baseline (exp-015) | Yes (Section 6.2) | Adequate |\n| weighted_sc_baseline (exp-016) | Yes (Section 6.2) | Adequate |\n| temperature_ablation (what-if-temp) | Yes (Table 6, Section 5) | Adequate, n=60 |\n| case_studies (exp-008) | Partial (Section 6.4) | Text only, no figure |\n\nCoverage is generally good (16/16 experiments represented), but several lack rendered figures and some key conditions are underpowered or degenerate. The paper benefits from breadth but would benefit more from depth on 2-3 critical experiments.\n\n### Final Verdict\n\nThe paper has a solid empirical core (Tables 2-3) but needs significant revision in claim scoping, scholarship, and presentation before it meets the bar for a top ML venue. The mechanism analysis is publishable-quality work wrapped in over-generalized framing with critically thin references and missing figures. With the recommended revisions (especially items 1-5), this could become a solid contribution.","project":"symb_invariant_consensus","tier":"best","dream_id":"20260517T144412","overall_score":"3.5/6 Borderline","review_visible":true},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1551,"cache_re